# [What TCP is]($heading.id('what'))

TCP gives two programs a reliable, ordered stream of bytes over IP. Bytes
arrive in the order they were sent, and lost packets are sent again. TCP
doesn't encrypt anything, doesn't know who is on the other end, and carries
only one stream per connection. NomadFS adds those on top, with
[Noise]($link.page('learn/noise')) and [Yamux]($link.page('learn/yamux')).

A NomadFS node listens on one TCP port: `port` in `nomadfs.conf`, 9000 by
default. Every connection to it starts with a Noise handshake that must
finish within 10 seconds; a peer that connects and stays silent is dropped.
Connections are pooled: a device keeps one connection per peer and reuses it
for every request. A reaper thread closes connections that have been idle
for five minutes.

# [Where addresses come from]($heading.id('addresses'))

[Three sources of addresses lead to one routing table.](<$image.siteAsset('learn/reach.svg').alt('Three sources at the top: bootstrap_peers in nomadfs.conf (written by an invite or by hand), LAN announcements sent by UDP multicast every 2 seconds, and peers named in FIND_NODE replies from the DHT. Each leads to dialing TCP, the Noise handshake and Yamux; then a PING and PONG, where the node ID must match the handshake; then the routing table, with buckets 0 to 255 holding up to 20 peers each. To reach a device by ID: dial its address if the table has it, otherwise run FIND_NODE three peers at a time.')>)

**Bootstrap peers.** `bootstrap_peers = ["tcp://192.168.1.20:9000"]` lists
addresses to dial at start-up. Each one gets up to five attempts, a second
apart. When you join a swarm with a one-time invite, the app adds the
inviter's address to this list, so the two devices find each other again
after a restart.

**LAN announcements.** Every 2 seconds each node sends a small UDP datagram
to the multicast group `224.0.0.251` on port 5354. It holds the node's ID and
TCP port: `NMFS{"id":"…","port":9000}`. A node that hears an announcement
from an ID it doesn't know dials the sender's address on that port. The group
address is the one mDNS uses, but the message is not DNS: it is NomadFS's
own announcement, on its own port. Set `enable_mdns = false` to turn it off.

**The DHT.** Peers also learn about each other from the
[Kademlia](https://pdos.csail.mit.edu/~petar/papers/maymounkov-kademlia-lncs.pdf)
distributed hash table described below.

**Getting through a router.** With `upnp_enabled = true` (the default) the
node asks the local router over UPnP to forward its TCP port, and removes the
mapping when it stops. After it connects to a bootstrap peer, it sends a
`DIAL_BACK` request asking that peer to connect back to it, which shows
whether the port can be reached from outside.

# [Two kinds of ID]($heading.id('ids'))

A device has two key pairs, so it has two IDs:

| Key | Used for | ID |
| --- | --- | --- |
| X25519 transport key (`node.key`) | the Noise handshake | node ID = BLAKE2s-256 of the public key |
| Ed25519 identity key (`device.key`) | pairing, trust, revocation | device ID = BLAKE2s-256 of the public key |

The routing table is keyed by node ID. A node only adds a peer to it after a
`PING` on a live connection, and the ID in the `PONG` must equal the one the
handshake authenticated. A peer can't plant a false entry by claiming
someone else's ID.

# [The Kademlia DHT]($heading.id('kademlia'))

Kademlia arranges nodes by the *XOR distance* between their IDs: the
distance between `a` and `b` is `a XOR b`, read as a 256-bit number. IDs
that start with the same bits are close.

- **Buckets.** The routing table has 256 buckets. Bucket *i* holds peers
  whose ID shares exactly *i* leading bits with ours, up to K = 20 per
  bucket. Every bucket covers half the remaining ID space, so a node knows
  many peers close to it and a few far away.
- **Lookup.** To find a node, ask the closest peers you know for the peers
  *they* know closest to the target (`FIND_NODE`). NomadFS keeps up to 3
  queries in flight and repeats with the closer peers each round, until no
  one closer turns up. Peers named in replies are not added to the routing
  table until they answer a `PING` themselves.
- **Upkeep.** After bootstrap, a node looks up its own ID once, which fills
  its buckets. Every 60 seconds it refreshes the buckets.

The same protocol carries provider records (`ADD_PROVIDER`,
`GET_PROVIDERS`): a node can announce that it holds a file, so others can
find it by content. Shares and the swarm folder don't need this. They already
know which device holds a file: the one that shares it, or the one that added
it to the swarm folder.

# [Reaching a device]($heading.id('reaching'))

When NomadFS needs a particular device, for example to fetch a block of a
share it is browsing:

1. If the routing table has the device's node ID, it dials the addresses
   stored there (up to 8 per peer), or reuses the open connection.
2. If not, it runs a `FIND_NODE` lookup for that ID and then dials.

Every request after that is a message on a Yamux stream: one byte for the
message type, the sender's 32-byte node ID, then the payload. Requests
include `PING`, `FIND_NODE`, `GET_BLOCK`, `GET_SHARES`, `GET_SWARM`,
`REVOKE` and `ROTATE_KEY`.

Source: `src/net/transport/tcp.zig`, `manager.zig`, `mdns.zig`, `nat.zig`,
and `src/net/discovery/`.
